const sections = [];
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How much does chimpanzee trekking cost in Kibale?', a: 'A chimpanzee trekking permit at Kibale Forest National Park costs USD 250 per person. This includes park entry, a Uganda Wildlife Authority ranger guide and one hour of observation time with the chimpanzee community. The permit must be booked in advance through us or the Uganda Wildlife Authority.' },
  { q: 'Is chimpanzee trekking guaranteed in Kibale?', a: 'Kibale has a very high success rate for chimpanzee sightings — typically above 95%. The habituated communities are tracked daily by rangers and their location is known each morning. On rare occasions of forest fires, heavy rainfall or if the chimps have moved deep into the forest, the trek may be longer or the encounter shorter.' },
  { q: 'How does Kibale chimpanzee trekking compare to gorilla trekking?', a: 'Kibale\'s chimpanzee trek is generally easier and less physically demanding than gorilla trekking in Bwindi. The forest is lower altitude and less steep. Trekking times are usually 1 to 3 hours. The chimpanzee encounter is less intimate than gorillas — chimps move fast through the canopy — but the energy and noise of a chimp community in the forest is an extraordinary experience.' },
  { q: 'Can I combine Kibale with other Uganda parks?', a: 'Yes. Kibale is on the main road between Kampala and Queen Elizabeth National Park, making it a natural stop on any western Uganda safari circuit. Most popular combinations: Kibale + Queen Elizabeth (5 to 7 days), Kibale + Bwindi + Queen Elizabeth (7 to 10 days), or the full Uganda circuit including Murchison Falls.' },
];
